Concept: Behavioral Anti-Bot Checks with JS-Generated Tokens
Why?

    Bots and scripts often submit forms immediately without any user interaction.
    Humans almost always perform some interactions on the page: moving mouse, scrolling, typing, clicking.
    Capturing these user events and generating a token or flag lets your server validate whether the form submission likely came from a real user or a bot.

How does it work?

    At page load:
        JavaScript binds listeners for user interactions — scroll, mousemove, keydown, click.
        Upon the first such interaction, JS injects or enables a hidden input field with a token or sets a flag value in a hidden field.

    Timing enforcement:
        Additionally, measure time elapsed since the page was loaded.
        Detect if a form was submitted too quickly (e.g., within 2 seconds), flag as suspicious.

    On form submit:
        The token or interaction flag is sent with the POST data.
        The server validates:
            Is the token present?
            Was the token generated properly (optionally signed, timestamped)?
            Has enough time passed before submission?

    If validation fails:
        Reject the submission or treat as bot.
        Log, throttle, or challenge user further (captcha, etc.).

Implementation details
